WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.230
All right, let's go.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.770
2012, DeLibor from the Czech Republic.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:15.370
Good luck, DeLibor.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.690
And we have another Sicilian and we

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.230
have faced virtually no open Sicilians.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:22.510
This is, this is a plague on humanity.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.410
Everybody's going night C3 now.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:25.330
It's crazy.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.030
And we've already faced this move like

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.190
three or four times, but hey, this is a

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:33.110
realistic cross section of what people

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:33.690
play nowadays.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.950
Opening fashions change.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.030
I feel like even like two, three years

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.510
ago, open Sicilian was, you know,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.970
everybody was playing it and the people

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.270
who weren't were playing the Aleppan.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.570
Now people are going for all sorts of

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.430
night C3 variations.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.230
We're going to respond with a move that

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:52.010
I talked about previously, rather than

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.030
going night C6.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.990
And the downside of night C6 is that it

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.290
allows this incredibly tricky sideline,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.050
Bishop B5.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:00.570
We've already faced this sideline.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.390
I've gotten into trouble against it.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.490
And around that time, I talked about

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.910
the immediate G6, which is very

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.210
uncommon, but it sidesteps a lot of

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:09.770
annoying lines.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.610
If white is a grand pre -attack player,

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.750
then it doesn't really matter what you play.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.470
You can play night C6 or G6.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.810
Our opponent is a grand pre -attack

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.910
player, which is night C3 followed by F4.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.730
The grand pre -attack is one of the most

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.770
dangerous systems against the Sicilian.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.350
I haven't refreshed my own theory in a

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:27.850
long time.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.050
So this is going to be really fun.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.430
Let's see if we can avoid getting checkmated.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.190
First things first.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.310
Let's fianchetto our Bishop.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.110
And usually white plays night F3.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.590
And in this position, we have several

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:39.350
different setups.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.250
Essentially, we can start by playing

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.550
D6, or we can start with a more

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.850
conventional night to C6.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.350
Let's play more conventionally with

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.150
night C6.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.930
And Bishop C4 is the old school move.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.550
The new school move is Bishop to B5.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.670
This is a more positional way of

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:57.990
playing the grand pre -attack.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.470
Bishop C4 is the sort of old school

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:03.190
method where you're aiming directly at F7.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:05.410
And you can watch my previous videos

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.050
for more of a background on the grand

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.710
pre -attack, because this is a higher

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:09.730
level game.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.790
I'm going to skip straight to the sort

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.690
of juice and talk about the moves themselves.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.310
Afterward, we'll do a little bit more

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.410
theoretical investigating.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:18.830
Okay.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:22.330
So as far as I know, the most popular

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.350
setup, and this was popularized many

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.270
decades ago, is what was taught to me

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.290
as the Botvinnik setup.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.530
And that is where you go E6, and then

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.030
you'd put the knight on E7.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.630
It's essentially like Fianchetto,

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.930
knight on E7, knight on C6, and you can

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.010
do it with white as well.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.570
This is a setup, this is a system, not

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.010
necessarily a specific setup against a

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:44.850
particular opening.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.610
E6, as far as I remember, there's this

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.390
very dangerous gambit line where white

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.570
responds with D4.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.630
So there's a lot of theory associated

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:53.950
with the move E6.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.850
And because I haven't refreshed my

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.790
knowledge in a long time, I'm inclined

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.630
to play a slightly more modest move.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.050
Maybe we can start with D6, and then we

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.590
can either play E6 or we can play a

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.250
line that I introduced a couple of

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.770
speedrun games ago, where we bring our

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.470
knight actually out to H6.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.450
This is an also interesting line

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.530
against the grand pre -attack.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.910
Let me think about what we want to do here.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:20.830
So E6, I don't really want to play, but

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:21.470
we can.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.270
We can start with D6 and then play E6.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.530
That's totally possible.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:27.150
Let's do that.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.550
Let's start with D6.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.610
And now that we have opened up our

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.150
bishop, one thing that we don't want to

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.970
do here is play Bishop G4.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.810
This is a terrible move that walks

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.370
right into this very well -known tactic.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.270
Bishop takes F7 check, followed by

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:41.810
Knight G5.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.250
So definitely not Bishop G4.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.290
We can play Knight F6, but it was

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.750
taught to me a very long time ago that

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.970
you want to avoid bringing your knight

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:51.930
out to F6 in the grand pre -attack and

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.550
in the closed Sicilian, I'll talk a

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.330
little bit more about that after the

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.290
game, I think E6 is a much safer

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.090
option, and it's an option that makes

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.590
it more difficult for white to

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.070
jumpstart his Kingside attack.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:07.450
So let's, let's go E6, Knight H6 maybe

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:08.850
is a move here, but with the bishop

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.210
already out on C4, there's also by the

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:12.090
way, the move Knight A5.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.230
I've seen this move played sometimes

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.490
Knight A5 in order to kick the bishop

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:19.710
away from this diagonal.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.030
So it should make logical sense that

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:23.650
you want, you would want to do that

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:26.810
because F7 is our weakest pawn, but

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:27.470
let's go E6.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.010
Let's, let's play more conventionally

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:29.530
in this game.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.590
I'm definitely out theoried.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:31.770
Okay.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.590
D4, this is a, this is getting a little

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:36.210
disturbing because D4 here, I'm not too

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:36.770
familiar with.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.330
So basically I'm on my own and this is

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.630
where I might get crushed if I do.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:42.050
Okay.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.330
But we're going to try to use general

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.070
opening principles to figure out our

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.430
way out of this opening.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:48.090
Okay.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.110
So the idea of D4 is to blast open the center.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.050
And as far as I remember, after C takes

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.570
D4, White responds with Knight to B5.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.930
Uh, this is a very dangerous line and

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:58.930
we could get in trouble here.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.110
After CD4, White plays Knight B5 and

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.810
then White is exerting pressure on D6.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.110
But the main idea is that if we respond

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.190
to the move like A6, White takes the

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:09.890
pawn on D4.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:10.750
Okay.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.470
And once the D file is opened, the D6

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.850
pawn is quite a bit weaker.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.230
It's easier to attack.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:18.910
Now, I don't think we have much of a

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:19.270
choice here.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.550
We have to take this pawn.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.450
Maybe we could also take it with the

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.690
Knight, but I don't really see a reason

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.730
to give up our well -developed Knight.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.950
I think we should start with C takes D4

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.790
and now we should invest a little bit

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:30.110
of time.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.010
So one thing that I really don't want

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.450
to do is play A6 because what is

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.510
White's next move likely to be?

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.070
Well, likely it's going to be Knight

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.150
takes D4 anyway.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.090
So I think A6 is, I wouldn't say a

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.010
waste of time entirely, but I would

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.250
prefer to invest the tempo that we have

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:50.890
into a developing move.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.690
And there's only one logical developing move.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.510
The reason we played E6 is in order to

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.250
pave the way for Knight G to E7.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:05:59.910
And I think that that's what we should do.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.050
Let's go Knight G E7.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.310
Obviously our opponent's still in theory.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.890
So I don't like facing prep that's this

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.390
extensive Bishop B3.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:07.870
Okay.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.370
I understand why he did this though.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:12.730
The point of Bishop B3 is to take the

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.790
sting out of the move D5, which we were

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:15.410
sort of threatening.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.690
Now D5 can be met with E5.

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.810
Of course the Bishop is no longer hanging.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.390
And I don't want to play D5 E5 because

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.310
that creates big dark square holes,

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.610
both in the center and on the King side.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:27.690
I don't love D5.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.390
It's a move that we would play if we

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:29.850
were desperate.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.070
I think that we can essentially

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.010
continue developing.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.370
So I don't see a compelling reason that

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.810
we shouldn't castle King side.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.690
I mean, again, we could play A6, but I

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.130
don't really see the reason to do that.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:44.130
Yeah.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.170
I think we should just castle.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.410
And let's see how white responds.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.750
And this is very extensive preparation

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:51.470
by our opponents.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:52.410
So big, big kudos.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:52.710
Okay.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:54.690
So now we have a position that I know a

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.030
little bit better because this is

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.950
essentially an open Sicilian type structure.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.550
You can compare this to an accelerated drag.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:03.950
And the biggest difference is that here

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:05.010
our Knight is on E7.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.310
Here we have a pawn on E6 and both of

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:09.210
those things don't occur that often in

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:09.810
the accelerated.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.890
But by and large, in terms of the

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.410
structure in the center and the fact

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.410
that D4 has been played, we have a more

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.950
similar type of position to the open Sicilian.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.430
Now, how are we going to proceed?

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:21.750
Okay.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.810
Well, first of all, we have the option

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.370
to trade Knights, which could be good.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:26.990
Could be bad.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:27.750
It's hard to say.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.570
So we could trade and then bring the

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.730
other Knight out to C6.

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:34.310
Very typical idea in a lot of different

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.230
Sicilian structures.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.130
That's one option.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.890
Another option is to basically play it

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:42.230
more slowly, but I'm worried that white

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.070
will play F5.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:46.170
I'm really worried about this move F5,

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.230
which could really cause problems on

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:49.970
our King side.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.890
So there is Queen B6.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.430
There is Queen B6, but then white plays

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.630
Bishop E3 and our Queen really looks

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.350
dumb on B6.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.590
Another move that we could turn to is

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:02.330
Knight to A5.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.370
Maybe it's a good time to play Knight

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.610
A5 and get rid of this Bishop in order

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.730
to take the sting out of the move F5,

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.470
which otherwise could be really,

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:12.370
really, really scary.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.330
So I actually kind of like the look of

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:15.890
Knight A5.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.890
Yeah, let's try Knight A5.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.130
And hopefully this will finally take

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:21.250
our opponent out of theory.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:23.790
What I like about Knight A5 is it

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.310
doesn't, it's not a very committal move.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.290
So we can chill on A5 as long as we

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:28.710
need to.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:30.790
And then at the right moment, we could

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:31.890
take the Bishop on B3.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.710
Obviously like Bishop A4 is a super

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.770
awkward move and that Bishop could be

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:38.010
targeted with A6 B5.

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.870
So I'm not worried that white's going

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:40.490
to keep the Bishop.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.310
I'm not really worried about Knight B5.

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:44.790
That would be a really bad move because

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:45.790
of Queen B6 check.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.250
It would send the Knight back to D4

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:48.730
where we could pick it off with the

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:49.270
move E5.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.810
So I'm anticipating a developing move

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.990
like Bishop E3, and then we can breathe

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:55.710
a little bit easier because I know I

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:56.470
won't get checkmated.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:00.870
No, Alan Phillip, Queen B6 and E5 would

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.210
not have worked because there was this

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.530
move Knight D4 to F5, classic trick.

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:07.170
I'll show that after the game.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:08.930
I'll write it down actually.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:09.590
Okay.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.190
So what is going to be our kind of

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:15.350
system of development?

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.350
Well, when I look at this position, the

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:19.290
Bishop on C8 really needs to be deployed.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:21.970
And I think the best spot to deploy it

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:23.210
is to B7, right?

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.690
I mean, we could go Bishop to D7, but

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.870
then our pawn on D6 gets even weaker.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.070
And I feel like if we could bring our

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.950
Bishop to B7, at least we're exerting

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.870
some pressure on white's center.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.790
So here I would not rush with Knight

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.470
takes B3 because we could play this move.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:42.390
Whenever we want to.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:44.710
And of course, Knight takes B3 creates

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:45.670
a positional concession.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:46.930
It opens up the A file.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.110
So white will be able to develop some

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.630
pressure on our Queen side.

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.630
So I think that we should go on with B6

