I'm just going to promote.

01:09:38.570 --> 01:09:40.250
But hopefully you're spotting a

01:09:40.250 --> 01:09:41.510
pattern, which is your basic...

01:09:41.510 --> 01:09:42.790
How do you solve this?

01:09:43.130 --> 01:09:44.990
Well, the basic premise, the basic idea

01:09:44.990 --> 01:09:49.350
is that in the situation after Black

01:09:49.350 --> 01:09:51.650
takes on b1, the knight is essentially trapped.

01:09:52.410 --> 01:09:54.030
And if you keep calculating here, you

01:09:54.030 --> 01:09:56.710
might find that King c2 almost traps

01:09:56.710 --> 01:09:57.050
the knight.

01:09:57.610 --> 01:09:59.550
Here the knight is able to escape, but

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:01.030
only through b5.

01:10:01.230 --> 01:10:03.310
That's the only square that the knight

01:10:03.310 --> 01:10:04.290
is able to escape through.

01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:06.370
Otherwise, you would have to repeat

01:10:06.370 --> 01:10:07.070
moves forever.

01:10:07.210 --> 01:10:08.510
Knight b1, King c2, etc.

01:10:09.070 --> 01:10:11.470
So it's a prophylactic move that sets

01:10:11.470 --> 01:10:12.610
up the drawing mechanism.

01:10:13.550 --> 01:10:15.310
a4 is correct.

01:10:15.990 --> 01:10:16.710
a4.

01:10:17.150 --> 01:10:17.770
Knight a3.

01:10:18.250 --> 01:10:19.770
Now you walk the king over.

01:10:20.410 --> 01:10:20.990
Black promotes.

01:10:21.670 --> 01:10:22.490
You trade.

01:10:23.050 --> 01:10:25.530
And with b5 under your control, you're

01:10:25.530 --> 01:10:27.270
able to force a repetition of moves.

01:10:28.070 --> 01:10:28.650
King b3.

01:10:29.350 --> 01:10:30.670
And knight doesn't have b5.

01:10:30.790 --> 01:10:31.950
Knight has to go back to b1.

01:10:31.970 --> 01:10:33.910
Now not King b2, because then the

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:34.450
knight escapes.

01:10:34.690 --> 01:10:37.210
King c2 to keep the knight confined.

01:10:37.930 --> 01:10:38.690
And this is a draw.

01:10:40.350 --> 01:10:43.030
And that's how I could have drawn the game.

01:10:43.930 --> 01:10:44.930
Unbelievable.

01:10:45.250 --> 01:10:48.250
So the line is in this position, Bishop

01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:50.410
a8, bc, Bishop e4.

01:10:50.670 --> 01:10:51.730
This is the easy part.

01:10:52.470 --> 01:10:55.110
King e2 or a4, by the way, a4 even here

01:10:55.110 --> 01:10:55.490
is good.

01:10:55.730 --> 01:10:56.970
Doesn't matter what the order is.

01:10:57.330 --> 01:10:59.070
But you can't forget to play a4,

01:10:59.190 --> 01:11:00.890
because otherwise the knight sits on a3

01:11:00.890 --> 01:11:03.030
and your pawn is in immobilized.

01:11:03.330 --> 01:11:04.070
This is brilliant.

01:11:04.250 --> 01:11:04.930
a8, 4.

01:11:05.310 --> 01:11:06.250
Drawing move.

01:11:06.930 --> 01:11:08.130
This absolutely should be used.

01:11:08.230 --> 01:11:09.850
I'll use this in some video, because

01:11:09.850 --> 01:11:11.690
I'll eventually do a book or a

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:13.310
chessable course like best moments from

01:11:13.310 --> 01:11:15.390
speedrun series, most instructive

01:11:15.390 --> 01:11:16.470
moments, and this is one of them.

01:11:16.790 --> 01:11:17.970
This is unbelievable.

01:11:18.730 --> 01:11:19.710
I'd be one king c2.

01:11:19.970 --> 01:11:22.050
I hope you found this as beautiful as I did.

01:11:22.710 --> 01:11:24.310
So Bishop a8 could have drawn the game,

01:11:24.390 --> 01:11:25.750
but instead our opponent gives this

01:11:25.750 --> 01:11:26.470
tempting check.

01:11:26.830 --> 01:11:28.810
And now the problem is that if white

01:11:28.810 --> 01:11:30.430
tries to do the same thing, right, if

01:11:30.430 --> 01:11:33.070
white plays Bishop takes a8 now, well,

01:11:33.250 --> 01:11:35.470
it's not going to work anymore, because

01:11:35.470 --> 01:11:37.170
at the end of this line, what's the difference?

01:11:37.270 --> 01:11:38.470
Well, the difference is the bishop used

01:11:38.470 --> 01:11:39.150
to be on f1.

01:11:39.830 --> 01:11:42.090
Here black is not making us be the one

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:43.310
to take the rook with the king.

01:11:43.490 --> 01:11:44.910
With the bishop, instead we're taking

01:11:44.910 --> 01:11:46.890
the rook with the king, and white loses.

01:11:47.610 --> 01:11:50.090
So our opponent tried this move, but we

01:11:50.090 --> 01:11:51.770
win the game immediately with knight f3.

01:11:52.130 --> 01:11:54.090
Probably the most resilient would have

01:11:54.090 --> 01:11:56.650
been to play Bishop takes e4 check and

01:11:56.650 --> 01:11:57.510
Bishop takes e5.

01:11:57.550 --> 01:11:59.170
This would have kept white in the game,

01:11:59.630 --> 01:12:01.150
because here white has a pawn for the

01:12:01.150 --> 01:12:02.870
exchange, but black is still winning.

01:12:03.010 --> 01:12:03.750
Black is still winning.

01:12:04.310 --> 01:12:04.930
Not a piece.

01:12:05.050 --> 01:12:06.310
We are not winning a piece because of

01:12:06.310 --> 01:12:08.870
this check, but black should win this

01:12:08.870 --> 01:12:10.970
with technique, because the center is

01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:12.970
wide open, and the rook should prevail

01:12:12.970 --> 01:12:13.550
in this case.

01:12:14.110 --> 01:12:16.430
So this would have been the best chance

01:12:16.430 --> 01:12:21.790
for black, for white, but still, okay,

01:12:21.830 --> 01:12:23.230
we can't blame our opponent for

01:12:23.230 --> 01:12:24.870
collapsing with the rook e7.

01:12:25.010 --> 01:12:26.410
I almost missed knight f3 check.

01:12:26.470 --> 01:12:28.030
That's an easy move to forget about.

01:12:29.310 --> 01:12:31.630
And that, ladies and gentlemen, is one

01:12:31.630 --> 01:12:32.830
of the most complicated games that we

01:12:32.830 --> 01:12:33.130
have played.

01:12:33.170 --> 01:12:34.570
I had a feeling that we were going to

01:12:34.570 --> 01:12:35.810
get something good, and we did.

01:12:36.750 --> 01:12:40.190
So in conclusion, g6 is a good move

01:12:40.190 --> 01:12:41.270
against knight c3 if you're an

01:12:41.270 --> 01:12:42.030
accelerated player.

01:12:42.890 --> 01:12:44.910
Then everything here was correct.

01:12:45.050 --> 01:12:46.550
We followed theory.

01:12:47.050 --> 01:12:49.350
Now if you're a theory junkie, you can

01:12:49.350 --> 01:12:50.890
investigate e6 on your own.

01:12:51.190 --> 01:12:52.450
I'm not going to do that in this video,

01:12:52.490 --> 01:12:54.150
just because I want to keep things at a

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:54.810
normal length.

01:12:54.850 --> 01:12:56.550
The way we played was fine.

01:12:57.250 --> 01:12:58.810
Our opponent played this very dangerous

01:12:58.810 --> 01:12:59.970
modern line with d4.

01:13:00.330 --> 01:13:02.170
We responded to it correctly by

01:13:02.170 --> 01:13:02.970
developing our pieces.

01:13:03.110 --> 01:13:04.770
Bishop b3, by the way, is a nice move.

01:13:05.570 --> 01:13:07.950
And the idea is to take the sting out

01:13:07.950 --> 01:13:10.190
of the move d5, prophylaxis.

01:13:10.370 --> 01:13:12.290
We responded correctly with knight a5

01:13:12.290 --> 01:13:15.130
and b6, and this is still theory.

01:13:16.190 --> 01:13:17.810
This was our mistake, bishop a6.

01:13:17.890 --> 01:13:19.670
So the improvement is bishop b7.

01:13:20.470 --> 01:13:22.110
Then the improvement for our opponent

01:13:22.110 --> 01:13:23.570
would have been to play bishop g5.

01:13:24.050 --> 01:13:26.150
And then, of course, the situation

01:13:26.150 --> 01:13:27.170
remained very complicated.

01:13:27.590 --> 01:13:29.050
He gave up a piece here.

01:13:29.190 --> 01:13:31.210
Bishop f2 would have kept an unclear position.

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:35.190
And then, fast forward all the way to

01:13:35.190 --> 01:13:38.750
the end, the beautiful draw with bishop

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:40.450
takes a8 after b4.

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:42.430
Any questions?

01:13:43.170 --> 01:13:43.850
Any questions?

01:13:45.430 --> 01:13:45.850
Okay.

01:13:46.570 --> 01:13:48.230
You have said g6 and e6 is wrong.

01:13:48.290 --> 01:13:48.710
Why not now?

01:13:48.790 --> 01:13:49.650
So great question.

01:13:50.850 --> 01:13:53.090
In previous speedruns, I have claimed

01:13:53.090 --> 01:13:55.550
that in the Sicilian, you normally

01:13:55.550 --> 01:13:58.290
don't want to combine the moves g6 and e6.

01:13:58.590 --> 01:13:59.170
That is true.

01:13:59.270 --> 01:14:00.070
I have claimed that.

01:14:00.490 --> 01:14:02.270
But there is a major caveat, which is

01:14:02.270 --> 01:14:05.510
that, okay, positionally, it's quite

01:14:05.510 --> 01:14:07.110
dubious to combine g6 and e6.

01:14:07.170 --> 01:14:09.870
But normally, it all relates to whether

01:14:09.870 --> 01:14:11.790
or not your opponent can gain control

01:14:11.790 --> 01:14:12.730
of the d6 square.

01:14:13.490 --> 01:14:16.370
In this position, the d6 square is

01:14:16.370 --> 01:14:17.350
under black's control.

01:14:18.130 --> 01:14:18.370
All right?

01:14:19.010 --> 01:14:20.350
The d6 square is under black's control.

01:14:20.690 --> 01:14:22.330
But I could also show you a situation

01:14:22.330 --> 01:14:25.790
where black plays g6 and e6, and white

01:14:25.790 --> 01:14:29.610
is able to stick a queen on d6, or a

01:14:29.610 --> 01:14:31.490
knight on d6, or white is able to play

01:14:31.490 --> 01:14:34.070
e4, e5, and really clamp down on that square.

01:14:34.330 --> 01:14:36.370
That's when you want to be very careful

01:14:36.370 --> 01:14:37.510
about combining these two moves.

01:14:37.590 --> 01:14:40.130
Here's an example of a game of mine.

01:14:40.170 --> 01:14:41.430
I've shown this game many times.

01:14:41.690 --> 01:14:43.350
But I think it's the best illustration

01:14:43.350 --> 01:14:45.670
of when not to play e6 and g6.

01:14:46.590 --> 01:14:48.010
This will hopefully give you a little

01:14:48.010 --> 01:14:50.770
context on the difference between this

01:14:50.770 --> 01:14:55.150
game and situation where it's bad to

01:14:55.150 --> 01:14:56.070
combine these two moves.

01:14:56.430 --> 01:14:57.530
Old game of mine.

01:14:57.770 --> 01:14:58.830
I managed to win this game.

01:14:58.910 --> 01:14:59.570
But look at what happens.

01:15:00.170 --> 01:15:01.490
My opponent plays a smith mora.

01:15:01.830 --> 01:15:02.970
I made the mistake of taking.

01:15:03.770 --> 01:15:05.190
At that point, I had no idea what the

01:15:05.190 --> 01:15:05.670
theory was.

01:15:05.770 --> 01:15:07.150
So I decided to play g6.

01:15:08.270 --> 01:15:10.210
And here, I decided to make a genius

01:15:10.210 --> 01:15:12.030
move and play e6.

01:15:12.710 --> 01:15:14.650
So what is the chief difference between

01:15:14.650 --> 01:15:17.190
this version and the version that we

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:17.790
had in our game?

01:15:18.070 --> 01:15:20.050
Well, look at the fact that white has a

01:15:20.050 --> 01:15:22.550
direct line of fire to the d6 square.

01:15:22.950 --> 01:15:24.750
So he could have played queen d6

01:15:24.750 --> 01:15:26.890
immediately, but instead he starts with

01:15:26.890 --> 01:15:28.870
knight b5, and that's it.

01:15:28.990 --> 01:15:31.030
Already, d6 is under white's complete control.

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:33.690
But instead of giving the check on d6,

01:15:33.750 --> 01:15:34.890
which a lot of people would have done,

01:15:35.070 --> 01:15:36.950
he plays queen to d6, which wins the

01:15:36.950 --> 01:15:39.730
game, because knight c7 is literally unstoppable.

01:15:39.810 --> 01:15:41.590
You can't do anything about that move.

01:15:42.170 --> 01:15:44.390
I had to play queen e7, and I ended up

01:15:44.390 --> 01:15:45.670
losing a full rook.

01:15:45.870 --> 01:15:47.250
I win this game because I ultimately

01:15:47.250 --> 01:15:49.810
end up winning the knight back, and I

01:15:49.810 --> 01:15:51.090
also grabbed this pawn in the center.

01:15:51.130 --> 01:15:52.670
So it's not that easy to convert, but

01:15:52.670 --> 01:15:53.610
you get the point.

01:15:54.030 --> 01:15:55.150
You get the point.

01:15:55.910 --> 01:15:58.690
It's all about whether the center is open.

01:15:59.690 --> 01:16:02.870
Also, you have to be very careful in a

01:16:02.870 --> 01:16:05.270
situation where white is able to play

01:16:05.270 --> 01:16:07.190
the move e5 and clamp down on the d6 square.

